Cassini
===========

Cassini is a server-stateful Tryton web client built with Voyager,
Dominate and HTMX.

The browser is intentionally thin. Each workspace, open tab, search, selected
record, view and unsaved field value is persisted in ``cassini.workspace``.
Reloading a browser therefore reconstructs the complete interface.

Client capabilities
-------------------

The client provides Tryton authentication (including authentication
challenges), logout, the user menu, global search and server-persistent user
preferences. Tryton actions open as persistent tabs. Supported action types
include window actions, wizards, reports and URLs.

Window actions support tree views (read-only and editable), form, list-form
and day, week and month calendar views. The renderer handles the form and tree
elements and attributes from Tryton's Relax NG schemas: states, notebooks,
groups and panes, prefixes and suffixes, optional and hierarchical columns,
totals, sequences, visual states, nested grid dimensions, expandable groups,
factors, symbols, scan-code forms, domain tabs, sorting, pagination and
calendar colours. Field values use Tryton's native types and the form lifecycle
combines defaults, ``on_change`` and ``on_change_with`` in one server request.
Relation widgets use Tryton's ``autocomplete`` RPC and provide open/create
actions; a record created from a relation is linked back into the unfinished
origin record. One2many and many2many fields use Sao-style embedded panels
with their own tree/form switch, current-position badge, previous/next
navigation and create, open, delete and undelete actions.

Form fields do not invent labels: only an XML ``label`` element renders one.
Required labels are bold and optional labels use normal weight, without adding
an asterisk.

Records can be loaded, selected, created, edited, saved in batches, reverted,
duplicated and deleted. The toolbar also exposes model buttons, related
actions, reports, attachments, notes, logs, revisions, CSV import and generic
or predefined CSV exports. It follows Sao's icon order and groups action,
relate, print and export entries in pop-up menus. Search occupies its own wide
row below those actions, with field-aware suggestions, a field filter pop-up,
user search bookmarks and record navigation. Optional tree columns use Sao's
hamburger menu, with select-all immediately below it. A single row click
selects it and a double click opens its alternate view, while clicks on fields
and controls retain their own behaviour. Hierarchical trees and menu branches
start collapsed and progressively expand with the same arrow icons. Binary
values and generated reports use dedicated download endpoints.

The shell has a persistent three-button control for no side panel, the menu
and the help panel. The buttons are mutually exclusive and show the selected
state. Both panels open on the left and can be resized horizontally, following
Sao's desktop layout, and always end exactly at the bottom of the viewport
with their own scrolling content. The global search sits immediately beside
this control and uses the same localized
``Search or chat`` wording as ``nantic_connection``. The light/dark theme is
compiled with Tailwind CSS. Core Tryton notifications, preferences, help and
logout are grouped in the icon-based user menu.

The help panel follows the accordion, layout and icon set from
``roots/private/sao/custom.js``. It includes the assistant, contextual
documentation, updates and support tickets. The assistant supports persisted
and NaN-specific conversations, Markdown responses, confirmation requests,
file, clipboard and drag-and-drop attachments, screenshots, screen recordings,
speech recognition, text-to-speech and conversation artifacts. Enter sends a
message while Shift+Enter inserts a line break. Remote assistance uses the same
confirmation flow and lazily initializes Surfly only after it is accepted.
When ``nantic_connection`` is activated, the assistant talks directly to
``nantic.chat.conversation`` and sends the active model, current record,
selection, domain, context, company and language. The conversation identifier,
expanded menu branches, active accordion section and panel state survive
reloads.

State and fragment model
------------------------

Every field mutation is sent to the server, including fields without an
``on_change``. Text input uses a cancellable debounce: a newer keystroke
replaces the queued request. When a response affects the component that
contains the focused control, the control's live node, value, focus and
selection are preserved; controls which are no longer focused accept the
server-rendered value. A workspace row is locked before it is read and updated, so
concurrent requests from different browser tabs cannot overwrite newer state.
All state-dependent endpoints explicitly disable rendered-component caching.
Voyager's cache remains enabled for immutable components and per-user
preferences. The route map is built once per database/site prefix instead of
being rebuilt on every HTMX request; Tryton's own invalidated caches remain
the authority for views, fields, toolbars and access rules.

Every response includes ``Server-Timing`` and ``X-Cassini-Ms``. The
Playwright warm-action regression test rejects a cached tab opening that takes
50 ms or more, while keeping roughly 7 ms as the optimisation target.

Endpoints normally return the smallest affected Dominate fragment. Related
fragments use HTMX out-of-band swaps. Multi-fragment screen and field responses
are streamed with buffering disabled, so an already rendered fragment can
leave the server while later fragments are being prepared. The included
JavaScript fallback implements the required HTMX subset for environments where
the CDN is unavailable.

Voyager endpoint URLs may carry typed component values in their query string.
The client binds those values before rendering, which keeps row buttons,
open-row navigation, optional columns, selections and configured exports
consistent for both HTMX and the JavaScript fallback. All components rendered
during one HTTP request reuse the same locked state engine.

Reusable infrastructure
-----------------------

``state.py`` contains the framework-neutral state document, serialisation,
component registry and fragment response helpers. Applications which do not
use Tryton XML views can register a component renderer with
``register_state_component`` and store their state in the same workspace.
The ``StatefulComponent`` base class exposes persistent component state, while
the ``/component/<name>`` endpoints render a registered component and merge
JSON state updates without requiring an XML view.

``engine.py`` adapts Tryton actions, models, defaults, on-change methods,
wizards and reports to that state document.

``/<database>/cassini/demo`` is a small complete application made only
from custom Dominate components. Its draft title, counter and task components
use the same state and fragment infrastructure and deliberately contain no XML
view. It is both a runnable example and a template for new websites.

The Tailwind output is reproducible from the module directory with::

    npx tailwindcss@3.4.17 -c tailwind.config.js \
        -i static/tailwind-input.css -o static/tailwind-output.css --minify

Usage and tests
---------------

After activating the module, the web client is available below
``/<database>/cassini/``. The first request creates a Voyager site of type
``cassini`` when the database does not already contain one.

The scenario suite combines integration tests and Playwright browser tests for
workspace type preservation, login/logout, CRUD, editable trees, multiple
persistent drafts, defaults and on-change, preferences, wizards, reports,
downloads, exports, imports and record actions. A browser matrix covers every
Sao form widget name, row and instance buttons, multiple selection, optional
columns, form/list-form/calendar views, view switching, relation
completion/creation, revisions, editable calendars, calendar modes and
scan-code submission. A dedicated tree scenario covers collapsed hierarchy,
persistent expansion, visual states, totals and sequence reordering. A
separate browser scenario proves that a custom component without an XML view
persists typed state through its generic endpoint. The shell/demo scenario
covers the Sao toolbar ordering and icons, column hamburger, search completion,
theme and shell persistence, welcome/help panels and the complete no-XML
application with unfinished drafts.
